<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borommarachathirat I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1370">1370</a>–<a href="/years/1388">1388</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Khun Luang Pa-ngua, Khun Luang Pa-ngua of Suphanburi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Borommarachathirat I, earlier Khun Luang Pa-ngua, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphanburi</a>, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> from 1370 to 1388. A brother-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i">U Thong</a>, the founder of Ayutthaya, he seized the throne from U Thong's son Ramesuan and established the Suphannaphum dynasty in power. He campaigned repeatedly against Sukhothai, which became a tributary of Ayutthaya in 1378.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borommarachathirat-i.jpg" alt="Borommarachathirat I" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FrameHotep · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borommarachathirat_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The name Ngua traditionally refers to the fifth-born child in archaic Thai numbering, so it is hypothesised that he was the fifth son of his family. As the elder brother of the consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i">Ramathibodi I</a>, he was formally addressed by that king as an elder brother. When Ramathibodi I established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> in 1350, he gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommaracha-i">Khun Luang Pha Ngua</a> the title Somdet Phra Borommarachathirat and sent him to rule the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphan Buri</a>.</p><p>In 1353 he marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> to support Ramesuan, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i">Ramathibodi I</a> had sent against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> king after his defection. The Khmer forces had routed the Ayutthayan vanguard, and the king summoned his brother-in-law from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphan Buri</a> to help. The campaign lasted about a year and ended in an Ayutthayan victory and the forced relocation of a large number of Khmer captives to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1370 he marched his army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphan Buri</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>. Ramesuan came out to welcome his maternal uncle, then abdicated and returned to govern Lopburi. Traditional chronicles describe a peaceful abdication, but historians widely interpret the arrival of an armed force as what pressed Ramesuan to step down, and see the change as a shift of power from the Lopburi faction of the Uthong dynasty to the Suphan Buri faction.</p><p>From 1374 he campaigned against northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamphaeng-phet">Chakangrao</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, Chiang Mai and Lampang, until the end of his reign. He led four expeditions against Chakangrao, modern Kamphaeng Phet, a frontier city of the Sukhothai Kingdom: in 1376 its ruler Phraya Sai Kaeo was killed, and in 1378 Thao Pha Khong fled and many nobles and commanders were captured. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-thammaracha-ii">Maha Thammaracha II</a> of Sukhothai later surrendered and was allowed to rule as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. An invasion of Chiang Mai was repulsed at the Battle of Sen Sanuk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1375, after consulting the monk Phra Maha Thera Thammakanyan, he commissioned Phra Si Rattana Mahathat in the eastern quarter of the city, the present Wat Mahathat, whose central prang stood 19 wa, about 38 metres, high. After his death in 1388 his son Thong Lan reigned for only a week before Ramesuan returned from Lavo, overthrew him and began a second reign. The dominance of the Suphannaphum dynasty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> that he began lasted for several decades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Borommarachathirat I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After U Thong founded Ayutthaya in 1351, <b>Khun Luang Pa-ngua of Suphanburi</b>, his brother-in-law, seized the Ayutthaya throne in 1370 as Borommarachathirat I and campaigned against Sukhothai.</blockquote><a href="/state/suphannaphum">Suphannaphum</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphan Buri</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in Phra Nakhon Si Ayutthaya, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.47,100.12&amp;year=1379" aria-label="Suphan Buri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7289 1625 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Borommarachathirat I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7739 1895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7739 1895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.47,100.12&amp;year=1379">What was here in 1379? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
